    "media:imagetext": "- 3 -\nThe dock leads into a small plaza formed by the non-palacial\npalace on the right, Government building and Post Offlee opposite the\npier, and real estate (subject to improvement) on the left.\nThia\nplaza and the pier were teeming with people--99% black--when we cane\nashore.\nThe few whites had all the places of vantage and even the\nbronsed Portuguese looked snow-white in comparison with the stove-\nMany of the latter wore only gee-strings.\nOne of thoge had approached our Chief Mechanie a few weeks ago and in\nperfect Oxfordian English lad offered to asaist in any way that he\ncould--Finally indicating that ve need only ask for \"Thunderbolt MeCoy\".\nHovever, he was not around when we landed.\nI suppose Mr. WIllklo welcomed the Irat complete exeuption\ntron reportera that he\nhag had in many, many noaths! At least he\nscened to enjoy thoroughly every minute of his stay in Bolane. The\nPortuguese offleials were, of course, profoundly impressed and hardly\nInew what to do. The coming of the Clipper was enough of an event to\nstun them, but when ir, #111kle and Mr. Trippe were added it was just\ntoo much.\nThe guard of honor Portuguese Major and ten jet black\nnatives in faded khaki uniforns, red lez hate, and bare beaver-tail-\nshaped feet-stood at attention while we trooped into the Palace grounds\nfor a photograph. Later an autonobile trip around tom and to \u0026 nearby\nnative village.\nWe met the obief in a ones-white suit, and twelve of\nhis twenty-six wives in no sulta at all (the rest were working in the\npeanut fields). Later still, a reception and tea at the Pelace and then-\nwhat with the first flight covers to be cancelled and other jobe incident\nto a firet flight-nothing ever runa snoothly--lt vas too dark to take\noff and so we decided to stay another day. Back we went from the Dixie\nC11pper to the Company' staff house.\ngot together a dinner for us which was very good- it fru-\ngal. I sat at a onall table with irs. Long, wife of our Bolame airport\nmanager, Mr. #1l1kie, and Mr. Trippe.\nNaturally, I never expected to\ndins 1n darkest Africa with or without Mr. Willkie, nor least of all,\nI suapeet, did Mrs. Long. She was the daughter of \u0026 Beotchen who spent\nmany yeere at Horta.\nThere she ast and married Long, and they Beened\nto be an ideal couple to send to Bolena for both speak Portuguese.\nHe had enough beds for all at the staff house.\nThis morning\nwe boarded a small steambost as the gueste of the Governor, went to the\nmainland and there rode on Ford trucks back into the interior sixty\nmiles.\nWish you could have seen me sitting in that truck on a box with\na sun helmet on my heed and an Army rifle over my knees while we rettled\nover a winding red clay road through the jungle. One of the C.A.A. nen\no us ali be both or hot oil.\nwell sa I can.\nIt was something of a strain linguietieslly and physically.\nMy rifle, which weighed about 20 Iba., was a \"throe nan gun\"-one man\nholde the rifle and two men\nhold him. Fortunately we only saw nany\nmonkeys and one small geselle which was out of range. I say \"fortunately\"\nfor I wouldn't have shot that gun for all the tea in Chine,\nSETONSAPI",
